Candidate 512 has 208 retained samples, main 208 and published 200; all 1024 arms have 128. The dense median gaps recur through ordinary blocks. The old candidate 113 ms block did not recur in this sitting, which proves neither its cause nor permanent prevention. p90 and p95 above are retained for all arms and interpreted symmetrically; no tail is silently discarded. The fixtures differ beyond label count, so their timings do not establish count scaling.

## Exclusions, calibration and scope

Main matches all 145 identities: Labs classifies 81 faster, 33 slower, 31 neutral and **zero skipped**. Published has **143 comparable identities**, classified 99 faster, 28 slower, 16 neutral and **zero skipped among those 143**. Its two documented unavailable outlines are “copy per-glyph metrics with outlines after text change” and “read every outline from an unchanged copy”; neither is an implicit parity pass. Both comparison manifests have zero baseline-check failures and zero timing-mode mismatches. Neutral means inconclusive at the runner's resolution, not verified parity. No included row was dropped to improve the decision.

The candidate report flags 56 of 145 identities as noisy within a process and 34 for between-block spread, with 5.9% software-calibration drift. The published comparison warns of **77.0% software-calibration drift**; its saved stress-file run/post rate also changes markedly while its eight dense-1024 block medians remain in the ordinary range. Main has no global drift warning, and its dense blocks are steady. These are software-loop diagnostics, **not measured hardware GHz**, and they do not identify contention, invalidate a chosen unfavorable sample, or prove equality of run conditions. Published's warning limits confidence in cleanly attributing its arm alone; the independently stable main dense losses already prevent strict parity. The published 512 batching-file run/post diagnostic is much smaller, but its apparent stability does not erase the global warning. An unrelated Vite process remained active; preflight idle snapshots do not prove core placement or absence of contention.

The seventh family keeps tails, memory and delivery in view. Candidate shaper-owned Rust requested allocation accounting exists for named operations, but its main diagnostic overlay did not reproduce shipping bytes, leaving it **unpaired**. It excludes JavaScript/GPU buffers and allocator fragmentation; Wasm capacity is reserved linear memory, not requested live allocation. No RSS, JS heap or whole-process total is admitted as owned-memory evidence. The separate actual-browser CPU diagnostic found smaller dense median gaps to main in its different fixture, but it cannot replace the seven-family Node matrix or measure GPU execution. Candidate cold-output and final-projection oracles passed; the editorial strict PNG comparator remains failed at 842 pixels with maximum channel difference 1, traced to a pre-wave block-rounding difference. This diagnostic remains explicit rather than rebaselined.

The corrected fixed-20 matrix is sufficient to reject the current candidate's **verified parity claim** because ordinary dense complete-operation medians lose to main and published, alongside other family losses. It does not identify the old spike's cause, license a tail-only rejection, establish a permanent warmup fix, or justify a new product algorithm from noise. The stack #292 → #301 → #302 → draft #310 remains held; this review performed no build, benchmark, gate, browser run, source change, or external action.

Evidence: fixed-warmup-freeze.json; matrix-fixed20-{candidate,main,published}/{manifest.json,candidate.json,baseline.json,summary.md,comparison.txt} where present; fixed20-admission-analysis.json; published runner receipt boundary-20261022-matrix-fixed20-published/result.json; current benches/reports/held-single-owner-local-2026-10-22/README.md and the named fixture sources.
